WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
Part 4. You will hear part of a food science lecture on fashions in food.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:14.680
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:26.320
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.320
There are trends in food much as there are trends in clothing.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.639
Interest in food fashions has risen rapidly since the birth of the smartphone, when people

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:46.199
first began taking photos of their food and instantly sharing them with their friends.

00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:53.720
The food industry in the UK in particular is obsessed with finding and exploiting the

00:01:53.720 --> 00:02:02.919
next big food trend. Marketeers aim to create a huge demand for a food item which was previously

00:02:02.919 --> 00:02:09.139
unknown or not needed. One of the most effective ways of promoting

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:17.160
a new food product is by using social media influencers as brand ambassadors. In return

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:24.020
for free samples, many influencers will post content about a product, although there are

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:30.080
influencers with hundreds of thousands of followers who can command large fees for their

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:37.759
services. Companies which sell vegan produce were pioneers in being able to increase sales

00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:44.880
really quickly in this way. For a food item to become really popular,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.720
it has to be readily available, so supermarkets have a role to play in creating a new food

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:59.960
trend. They have dedicated teams closely following which new products or ingredients are trending

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:06.800
on social media and are particularly interested in what well-known chefs are putting on their

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:15.919
menus. I'd like to look at a few examples of marketing

00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:23.000
campaigns which were really successful in launching a new fashion trend.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.399
Starting in the 1990s with the avocado, a British PR company was hired to raise its

00:03:30.399 --> 00:03:38.399
profile and stimulate demand. They paid for a group of journalists to travel out to South

00:03:38.399 --> 00:03:46.839
Africa to meet avocado farmers. Articles written following this visit helped to educate the

00:03:46.839 --> 00:03:53.880
British public about the avocado, which at this time was certainly not the daily staple

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:02.720
it's since become. Advertisements were designed to promote the avocado as a superfood, rich

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:11.039
in nutrients and therefore beneficial for health. Avocados became hugely fashionable

00:04:11.039 --> 00:04:19.640
and within a few years, UK avocado sales had grown from £13 million annually to around

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:30.119
£150 million, making it one of the most successful fresh produce campaigns in UK history.

00:04:30.119 --> 00:04:38.119
Oat milk is a recent example of a new product which became fashionable very quickly. Now

00:04:38.119 --> 00:04:45.480
there are many brands available, but one company which had early success was the Swedish brand

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:53.320
Oatly. They attracted a lot of attention with a media campaign which used provocation

00:04:53.320 --> 00:05:01.679
as a way of getting their message across effectively. The fact that this campaign aggravated competitors

00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:09.079
producing milk from dairy cows was seen as a plus, as it helped to make oat milk seem

00:05:09.079 --> 00:05:17.920
cool. In the USA, the brand decided against a big retail launch in favour of getting the

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:25.200
product into coffee chains, which removed the need for a big advertising budget. This

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:33.359
proved far more effective than offering samples in supermarkets. Oat milk had an advantage

00:05:33.359 --> 00:05:40.480
over other alternative milk products, such as almond milk. Many consumers prefer it

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.959
because it has less of an impact on the environment. It requires significantly less water to produce

00:05:47.959 --> 00:05:57.440
than other alternative milk products and it also has a relatively low carbon footprint.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:05.559
Norwegian Skre, a rarely available seasonal fish delicacy, otherwise known as Arctic Cod,

00:06:05.559 --> 00:06:12.239
is now found on the menus of Michelin-starred restaurants throughout Europe. The demand

00:06:12.239 --> 00:06:19.359
for Skre has been used by a food marketing agency to build the reputation of Norway's

00:06:19.359 --> 00:06:26.239
fisheries in general. Marketing surveys have shown that a significant number of shoppers

00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:34.679
now associate Norway with excellent seafood. Food trends can be considered a good thing

00:06:34.679 --> 00:06:42.679
in some ways, as they can benefit farmers and food producers enormously. The public

00:06:42.679 --> 00:06:49.420
can also be encouraged to buy things which are more sustainably produced, but ethical

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:57.260
concerns have been raised about the effects a surge in demand can cause. Quinoa is a classic

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:05.299
example. This plant is native to Peru, and when demand peaked some years ago, the price

00:07:05.299 --> 00:07:13.059
soared, making it unaffordable for local people. While the popularity of quinoa has benefited

00:07:13.059 --> 00:07:20.859
farmers financially, there have been other negative consequences. As demand grew, farmers

00:07:20.859 --> 00:07:28.779
began working the land all year round in order to produce more quinoa. One issue has been

00:07:28.779 --> 00:07:36.700
that the fertility of the soil decreased dramatically, which could potentially lead to desertification

00:07:36.739 --> 00:07:41.739
in some areas. Another example would be the case of...

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:51.500
That is the end of Part 4. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 4.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:57.700
That is the end of the listening test. In the IELTS test, you would now have 10 minutes

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.299
to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.

